WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
Statistical process control is a handful of

2
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.900
formulas. Each one is a picture.

3
00:00:05.983 --> 00:00:08.763
Two of those pictures carry the rest.

4
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.964
First idea. Control limits are learned from

5
00:00:13.064 --> 00:00:15.498
the process, not handed to you.

6
00:00:15.583 --> 00:00:19.402
This chart plots subgroup averages in sample order.

7
00:00:19.502 --> 00:00:22.323
The centre line is just their average.

8
00:00:22.416 --> 00:00:27.548
Now let the process talk. Each dot is one subgroup mean.

9
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:30.625
Their spread gives you sigma hat. Lines at

10
00:00:30.725 --> 00:00:34.085
three sigma either side are the control limits.

11
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:39.214
Limits come from the data. Specs come from the customer. Confusing

12
00:00:39.314 --> 00:00:43.179
the two is the most common mistake on a shop floor.

13
00:00:43.866 --> 00:00:47.226
Second idea. Capability is what happens when

14
00:00:47.326 --> 00:00:51.158
you compare the process against the specification.

15
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.022
Same process, now plotted against the measurement

16
00:00:55.122 --> 00:00:57.630
itself. This is where parts land.

17
00:00:57.716 --> 00:01:00.686
Now the customer's two limits. These

18
00:01:00.786 --> 00:01:04.096
were not calculated. They were dictated.

19
00:01:04.183 --> 00:01:09.435
Everything past them is scrap. The red area is how much to expect.

20
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:13.580
Nearest spec distance over three sigma is C p k.

21
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:17.641
About one, so a thousand bad parts per million.

22
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:21.656
The chart says whether the process is steady.

23
00:01:21.756 --> 00:01:25.053
Capability says whether steady is good enough.

